+\code{contrib/auto\_index/auto\_index.h}        & shared structs and GUCs declaration & 68 \\
+\code{contrib/auto\_index/auto\_index--1.0.sql} & SQL functions and catalog table & 37 \\
+\code{contrib/auto\_index/auto\_index.control}, \code{Makefile} & extension manifest, PGXS build & --- \\
+\code{scripts/\{build,run,benchmark,demo\}.sh}  & top-level driver scripts & $\sim$250 \\
+\code{scripts/lib/*.sh}                         & per-benchmark helpers (pgbench, TPC-C, TPC-H, strategies, setup) & $\sim$700 \\
+\code{scripts/tpch\_queries/q*.sql}             & 8 representative TPC-H queries & --- \\
+\code{scripts/tpcc\_xn/*.sql}, schema/load      & TPC-C-lite schema and the four transactions & --- \\
+\code{Dockerfile}, \code{docker-compose.yaml}   & reproducible build container & --- \\
+\bottomrule
+\end{tabular}
+\end{center}
+
+The C source is split into six clearly-labelled sections inside
+\code{auto\_index.c}: \code{1.\,MODULE INIT \& GUCs},
+\code{2.\,SHARED MEMORY}, \code{3.\,PARSING}, \code{4.\,STRATEGIES},
+\code{5.\,INDEX MANAGEMENT}, and \code{6.\,SQL-CALLABLE FUNCTIONS}.
+
+\section{How we tested}
+
+Four benchmarks, each scripted under \code{scripts/benchmark.sh}. All
+numbers come from a Docker container (Ubuntu 22.04, 6 cores, 8\,GB
+RAM, NVMe SSD), PostgreSQL 19devel built with \code{-O2
+--enable-debug --enable-cassert}. Each scenario was run three times.
+
+\paragraph{4.1\quad pgbench live demo.} 300\,K-row table, 4 clients
+$\times$ 2 worker threads, single equality predicate. TPS reported
+every 2\,s. Result: the bgworker fires at the second wake (\(t=6\)\,s)
+and TPS jumps from $\sim$340 to $\sim$17\,800 in a single window
+--- a $\sim$52$\times$ step, reproducible across all three runs.
+This is the strongest single demonstration that the extension is
+alive and correct.
+
+\paragraph{4.2\quad TPC-C-lite (OLTP).} 2 warehouses, pgbench-driven
+mix of NewOrder / Payment / OrderStatus / StockLevel. The single
+auto-created index was on \code{orders.o\_c\_id} (the OrderStatus
+join column); \code{auto\_index} correctly \emph{avoided} indexing
+customer columns despite heavy reads, because Payment also writes to
+those rows. Mean speedup 1.14$\times$ $\pm$ 0.02$\times$ across runs
+--- a small but correct OLTP result, capped by the bitmap-heap-scan
+limitation listed above.
+
+\paragraph{4.3\quad TPC-H SF=1 (analytical).} 6\,M-row \code{lineitem},
+8 representative queries (Q1, Q3, Q5, Q6, Q10, Q12, Q14, Q19). All
+six strategies were measured against the same baseline. The most
+aggressive strategy (\code{ratio\_only}) reached a geometric-mean
+speedup of \textbf{1.79$\times$}, with 3$\times$+ speedups on Q06,
+Q12, Q19 --- driven by the 3-column composite \code{(l\_quantity,
+l\_discount, l\_shipdate)} that exactly matches Q06's filter. The
+conservative default (\code{ski\_rental}) reached 1.53$\times$ with
+only 6 indexes built.
+
+\paragraph{4.4\quad Lifecycle demo.} 50\,K-row table; phase A is 200
+reads on a single column then 200 reads on a 3-column predicate;
+phase B is 300 INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE rounds with \emph{no} reads on
+the indexed columns. In every run, phase A produced one singleton
+plus one composite, and phase B dropped both within $\sim$20\,s
+(four bgworker cycles). 3/3 runs PASSED --- strongest correctness
+signal that the extension owns the full create-then-drop lifecycle.
+
+A late-stage docker-compose fix is also worth noting: PostgreSQL
+parallel hash joins back their shared hash tables with POSIX shm
+under \code{/dev/shm}, and Docker's 64\,MB default caused TPC-H
+Q10/Q12/Q14 to fail with ``could not resize shared memory
+segment''. We bumped \code{shm\_size:\,1g} in
+\code{docker-compose.yaml} and added a named volume for
+\code{/usr/local/pgsql} so future compose-file edits don't nuke the
+install.
+
+\section{How we used AI}
+
+We used \textbf{Anthropic Claude} (via Claude Code, models Opus 4.6 /
+4.7) extensively as a pair-programmer and a research assistant on
+PostgreSQL internals. AI was \emph{not} used to design the high-level
+approach (the ski-rental analogy, the strategy-pluggable architecture,
+the choice of which hook points to use) --- those came from team
+discussion and the original project proposal --- but the line-by-line
+C, the qual-extraction code, the benchmark scripts, and most of the
+PostgreSQL-internals research were AI-assisted.
+
+\paragraph{Representative prompts we used.} Condensed from real
+sessions; we kept transcripts in our local notes.
+
+\begin{itemize}
+\item \emph{Exploration of internals.} ``Show me where in the
+PostgreSQL executor the per-scan filter quals are stored at runtime
+--- I want to read them in an \code{ExecutorEnd} hook. Point me at
+the struct field and the file.''
+\item \emph{Hooking question.} ``What's the right way for a
+\code{contrib} extension to register an \code{ExecutorEnd\_hook}
+while playing nicely with chained extensions? Show the standard
+chain-and-restore idiom.''
+\item \emph{Shared memory.} ``I need a fixed-size shared-memory area
+sized at startup, with LWLocks, that holds an array of 256
+per-table records each containing a 32-slot column counter array.
+Show me the \code{\_PG\_init}-time \code{shmem\_request\_hook} +
+\code{shmem\_startup\_hook} pattern for PG 17/18.''
+\item \emph{Qual extraction.} ``Given a \code{List *quals} from a
+\code{SeqScanState}, write a recursive walker that handles
+\code{OpExpr}, \code{BoolExpr}, \code{ScalarArrayOpExpr}, and peels
+\code{RelabelType}. For each leaf, emit \code{(varattno, op\_kind)}
+where op\_kind $\in$ \{equality, range\}.''
+\item \emph{DDL safety.} ``What's the right way for a background
+worker to emit a \code{CREATE INDEX} (non-concurrent is fine)
+without breaking the transaction the user is in? SPI? An autonomous
+connection?''
+\item \emph{Composite-key ordering.} ``B-tree composite key: explain
+exactly which prefixes a multi-col B-tree can satisfy when the user
+queries \code{WHERE a = ? AND b > ? AND c = ?}.''
+\item \emph{Catalog tracking.} ``I want a small extension-owned
+table that survives \code{pg\_ctl restart} and lists every index
+this extension has created. What's the cleanest pattern --- install
+via the SQL install file?''
+\item \emph{Benchmark plumbing.} ``Write a bash script that runs the
+same TPC-H query set under each of six \code{auto\_index.create\_strategy}
+values, restarts the server between strategies, and emits a
+comparison table with per-query speedup and geomean.''
+\item \emph{Debugging.} ``PostgreSQL is failing TPC-H Q10 inside
+Docker with `could not resize shared memory segment to 16777216
+bytes: No space left on device'. What's the actual cause and the
+docker-compose fix?''
+\item \emph{Report drafting.} ``Write the implementation section of
+a 3-page project report from the following bullet list of facts
+about the extension. Keep it terse --- no headers per paragraph,
+no bullet-list filler.''
+\end{itemize}
+
+\paragraph{How we worked with AI in practice.}
+(1) \emph{Read first, generate second.} For PostgreSQL-internals
+questions we asked AI to find and explain the relevant upstream code
+(e.g.\ \code{src/backend/executor/execMain.c} around
+\code{ExecutorEnd}) before asking it to generate anything. We then
+read the upstream file ourselves.
+(2) \emph{Code was always reviewed and tested.} Every chunk of
+AI-generated C went through compile + run + regression of an earlier
+benchmark before being committed. Several AI-suggested approaches
+were rejected for being non-idiomatic (e.g.\ an early draft used
+\code{palloc} in shared memory; we caught it and rewrote with
+\code{ShmemAlloc}).
+(3) \emph{Benchmarks were authored manually first, then refined with
+AI.} The timing methodology and the ski-rental drop logic are ours;
+AI helped flesh out the bash plumbing and the median/geomean
+post-processing.
+(4) \emph{The report was AI-assisted.} Both the technical report
+(\code{report.tex}) and this submission report were drafted with AI
+help from team-supplied bullet points and benchmark CSVs, then edited
+by hand.
+
+We did not use AI for: the project topic / proposal, the choice of
+strategies, the decision to build a fork rather than a patch, or the
+specific test-data / benchmark choices.
+
